<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?> <?xml-stylesheet type="text/xsl" href="rss.xsl"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"> <channel> <title>Thor Henning Hetland</title><description>Software architect, AI development pioneer, founder of JavaZone and eXOReaction</description><link>https://wiki.totto.org/</link><atom:link href="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml" /> <managingEditor>Thor Henning Hetland</managingEditor><language>en</language> <pubDate>Tue, 14 Jul 2026 06:39:03 -0000</pubDate> <lastBuildDate>Tue, 14 Jul 2026 06:39:03 -0000</lastBuildDate> <ttl>1440</ttl> <generator>MkDocs RSS plugin - v1.19.0</generator> <image> <url>None</url> <title>Thor Henning Hetland</title> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/</link> </image> <item> <title>Getting Into the Phone Book of the Agentic Internet Took an Afternoon. Here&#39;s Why.</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>aeo</category> <category>agentic-web</category> <category>ed25519</category> <category>fly-io</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>mcp</category> <category>mcp-registry</category> <category>provenance</category> <category>streamable-http</category> <description>Both entries went live in the MCP registry in one working day: kcp-agent as tooling, and org.totto/knowledge — a person as a remote MCP server. The listing took an afternoon because the substance took months. Findable is table stakes; verifiable is the game.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/14/getting-into-the-phone-book-of-the-agentic-internet-took-an-afternoon-heres-why/</link> <pubDate>Tue, 14 Jul 2026 06:37:28 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/14/getting-into-the-phone-book-of-the-agentic-internet-took-an-afternoon-heres-why/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/phone-book-00-iceberg-overview.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>175 Posts, No Map</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>documentation</category> <category>dogfooding</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>knowledge-infrastructure</category> <category>navigation</category> <category>seo</category> <description>The site about the comprehension bottleneck hit its own comprehension bottleneck: 175 posts in six months and no way in. A week of renovation — entry paths, topic pillars, a 60,000-word field guide, and automation for every part that drifts.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/12/175-posts-no-map/</link> <pubDate>Sun, 12 Jul 2026 13:26:58 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/12/175-posts-no-map/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/kcp-two-repos-journey-map.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Two Halves of the Governance Problem</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>agentic-web</category> <category>compliance</category> <category>databricks</category> <category>defendable-agents</category> <category>governance</category> <category>kcp-harness</category> <category>meta-harness</category> <category>omnigent</category> <description>Databricks just open-sourced Omnigent, a meta-harness that governs AI agents with deterministic, session-aware policies enforced in code — not prompts. That is the exact bet behind kcp-harness and the Defendable Agents guide, now ratified by Matei Zaharia&#39;s team. But Omnigent and KCP govern different halves of the same problem: one governs what an agent does, the other governs what it knows — and lets you prove it later. Why that split matters, and why the two compose instead of compete.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/two-halves-of-the-governance-problem/</link> <pubDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 05:02:49 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/two-halves-of-the-governance-problem/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/kcp-agent-020-06-composes-with-mcp.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Notes From Inside the Harness</title> <author>Claude Code</author> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>defendable-agents</category> <category>dogfooding</category> <category>governance</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>kcp-harness</category> <category>mynder</category> <category>reflection</category> <description>A first-person field report from the AI collaborator: what it actually felt like to point kcp-agent at a real EU/Nordic regulatory knowledge web, watch a manifest that had fenced itself off from its own questions, get told &#39;no&#39; by a network policy, and then dogfood the whole thing against this very site — where my own manifest quietly failed its own test. Governance built for agents, experienced from the inside.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/notes-from-inside-the-harness/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 13:16:53 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/notes-from-inside-the-harness/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/kcp-agent-020-07-governance-imperative.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Google&#39;s Open Knowledge Format and the problems it deliberately doesn&#39;t solve</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>ai-agents</category> <category>google</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>knowledge-format</category> <category>okf</category> <category>temporal</category> <category>trust</category> <description>Google&#39;s Open Knowledge Format standardises content packaging for agents but stops before trust, temporality, and governance. KCP covers exactly that territory.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/17/googles-open-knowledge-format-and-the-problems-it-deliberately-doesnt-solve/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 12:56:22 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/17/googles-open-knowledge-format-and-the-problems-it-deliberately-doesnt-solve/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/blog/okf-kcp/okf-kcp-slide-04.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Defendable Agents</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI Agents & the Agentic Web</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Context Protocol</category> <category>ai-governance</category> <category>audit</category> <category>compliance</category> <category>defendable-agents</category> <category>determinism</category> <category>kcp</category> <category>kcp-agent</category> <category>prompt-injection</category> <description>The agentic web has a governance-shaped hole. Agents are about to read our regulations, spend our money, and brief our boards — and the dominant architecture can&#39;t answer &#39;why did you do that?&#39; with anything better than a chat log. Defendable agents can. Determinism at the core, the model at the edge, every decision evidenced.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/defendable-agents/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 09:50:53 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/07/08/defendable-agents/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/summer-plan-00-defendable-agent-workflow.webp" type="image/webp" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Clouded Vision</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>Software Architecture</category> <category>architecture</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>enterprise</category> <description>Cloud savings come from zero-admin architecture, not cheaper servers. Most apps carry enterprise DNA that makes migration painful without rethinking the design.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/clouded-vision/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/clouded-vision/</guid> </item> <item> <title>CloudMQ</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>Software Architecture</category> <category>architecture</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>eda</category> <category>messaging</category> <description>Platform-components-as-a-service will hit hard in 2009-2010, but without solid distributed-architecture understanding they add more pain than salvation.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/cloudmq/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/cloudmq/</guid> <enclosure url="https://wiki.totto.org/assets/images/cloud-eda.gif" type="image/gif" length="None" /> </item> <item> <title>Transparency is essential to security in the cloud</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>security</category> <category>transparency</category> <description>No infrastructure is secure in the absolute -- only for specific requirements. Why transparency, not vendor assurances, is the key to cloud security.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/04/20/transparency-is-essential-to-security-in-the-cloud/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/04/20/transparency-is-essential-to-security-in-the-cloud/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Cloud - auto-magic or just provisioning platform for your distributed system</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>Software Architecture</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>distributed-systems</category> <category>enterprise</category> <category>provisioning</category> <description>Clouds are provisioning platforms, not silver bullets. Enterprises still want magic, but the real question is whether to educate or go with the flow.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/05/07/cloud---auto-magic-or-just-provisioning-platform-for-your-distributed-system/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/05/07/cloud---auto-magic-or-just-provisioning-platform-for-your-distributed-system/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Rethinking Systems for AI</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>Software Architecture</category> <category>ai-architecture</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>data-infrastructure</category> <category>systems-thinking</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>AI asks why, not what. Systems designed for current-state queries need three shifts -- events over state, graphs over tables, conversations over queries.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/08/25/rethinking-systems-for-ai/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/08/25/rethinking-systems-for-ai/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Aurora: Answering Why</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>graph-database</category> <category>quadim</category> <category>root-cause-analysis</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>why-not-what</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>Aurora combines a temporal graph database with an AI query layer to answer why questions -- pattern detection, forensic investigation, and ripple effects.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/01/aurora-answering-why/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/01/aurora-answering-why/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Unlocking Temporal Graphs</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>bitemporal</category> <category>graph-database</category> <category>perfect-memory</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>A bitemporal model with four time dimensions -- transaction, valid, decision, and query -- enables perfect past recall and repeatable historical reporting.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/18/unlocking-temporal-graphs/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/18/unlocking-temporal-graphs/</guid> </item> <item> <title>From Data to Action: The Alchemy and Aurora Stack</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>Software Architecture</category> <category>alchemy</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>data-architecture</category> <category>reactive-systems</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>Alchemy handles reactive data ingestion via YAML config; Aurora stores temporal graph events. Two layers, independently scalable, cleanly separated.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/24/from-data-to-action-the-alchemy-and-aurora-stack/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/24/from-data-to-action-the-alchemy-and-aurora-stack/</guid> </item> <item> <title>The Organisational Amnesia Problem</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>Software Architecture</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>compliance</category> <category>data-architecture</category> <category>root-cause-analysis</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>Most systems overwrite history, making root cause analysis painfully manual. Temporal graphs make those questions answerable in seconds across six domains.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/10/10/the-organisational-amnesia-problem/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/10/10/the-organisational-amnesia-problem/</guid> </item> <item> <title>The Blind Spot of Now</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>amnesia</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>data-infrastructure</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>time-series</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>Current-state databases delete causation. Two case studies show how temporal analytics shifts operations from reactive to predictive.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/10/28/the-blind-spot-of-now/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/10/28/the-blind-spot-of-now/</guid> </item> <item> <title>The LLM Cautionary Tales</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>agentic-ai</category> <category>ai-security</category> <category>cautionary-tales</category> <category>horror</category> <category>llm</category> <category>prompt-injection</category> <category>security</category> <description>Seven dramatised LLM failure scenarios -- runaway loops, prompt injection, data leaks, lying evaluators -- written as horror stories so the lessons stick.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/11/05/the-llm-cautionary-tales/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/11/05/the-llm-cautionary-tales/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Mastering Deadlines: The 80/50 Rule</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>deadlines</category> <category>framework</category> <category>productivity</category> <category>project-management</category> <category>psychology</category> <category>time-management</category> <description>Parkinson&#39;s Law wastes early time; Student Syndrome wastes the rest. The 80/50 Rule hacks both by manufacturing urgency early and guarding the buffer.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/11/10/mastering-deadlines-the-8050-rule/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/11/10/mastering-deadlines-the-8050-rule/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Autonomy at Scale</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>Governance, Trust & Compliance</category> <category>async</category> <category>autonomy</category> <category>documentation</category> <category>exoreaction</category> <category>methodology</category> <category>organisation</category> <category>trust</category> <description>No meetings, no timesheets, documentation as default. Async-first organisation where trust replaces observation and clarity replaces coordination overhead.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/11/17/autonomy-at-scale/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/11/17/autonomy-at-scale/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Norway&#39;s Perfect Storm</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Knowledge Infrastructure</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>digital-transformation</category> <category>market</category> <category>norway</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>Industrial complexity, strict EU regulation, high digital maturity, and government AI investment make Norway a strong market for temporal analytics.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/12/12/norways-perfect-storm/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jul 2026 08:34:13 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/12/12/norways-perfect-storm/</guid> </item> </channel> </rss>